3. Research and explain in your own words what WPA2 and WPA3 are, and list two differences between them relevant to security.
=>WPA2 (Wi-Fi Protected Access 2) is the long-standing security standard used by most Wi-Fi networks since around 2004. It encrypts traffic using AES and authenticates devices with a pre-shared key (the Wi-Fi password) via a 4-way handshake.
WPA3 is the newer standard (finalized 2018, required for new Wi-Fi devices since 2020). It keeps AES encryption but significantly improves how devices prove they know the password and how session keys are created.
Two important security differences:

Resistance to offline password cracking
In WPA2 an attacker can capture the handshake once and then try millions of password guesses offline on a powerful computer.
WPA3 uses SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals). Every password guess must interact with the router in real time and is rate-limited, making brute-force attacks far harder.
Forward secrecy
With WPA2, if an attacker later learns the Wi-Fi password, they can decrypt previously recorded traffic.
WPA3 generates unique session keys that cannot be recovered from the password alone, so past traffic stays protected even if the password is later compromised.

4. Find a news article or real-world case from the past 3 years where a public Wi-Fi network was used in a cyber attack, and summarize how the attack happened and what could have prevented it.On your phone, turn on Mobile Hotspot / Personal Hotspot.
=>How the attack happened:

Attackers gained control of the captive-portal Wi-Fi gateway equipment used by hotels and conference centres.
They manipulated DNS and HTTP traffic so that users connecting to the legitimate public Wi-Fi were redirected to attacker-controlled pages.
Victims were shown fake Microsoft 365 login pages or fake “Windows update / ClickFix” prompts.
Credentials, session tokens, and malware (keyloggers, remote-access tools, information stealers) were delivered.
Corporate travellers’ Microsoft 365 accounts were targeted across multiple countries.

What could have prevented or reduced the damage:

Using a trusted VPN before doing any sensitive activity on public Wi-Fi.
Avoiding entering work credentials or clicking “update” prompts that appear on public networks.
Hotels and venues keeping their captive-portal equipment patched and monitored.
Enabling multi-factor authentication (MFA) on Microsoft 365 accounts so stolen passwords alone are not enough.
Preferring mobile data or a personal hotspot instead of public Wi-Fi for important work.
